      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 185,
      "text": "MEXICO\nAnnex V. Domestic Market Development, Foreign Investors and\nPeso Stability\nDespite continued portfolio outflows and tightening global financial conditions, the peso has been one\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 185,
      "end_char": 394,
      "text": "of the better performing currencies over the past year, even strengthening versus the dollar. This annex\nlooks at some of the drivers of the recent performance, including the importance of the increasing rate\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 394,
      "end_char": 587,
      "text": "differential to the U.S. Further, with foreigners continuing to reduce their holdings of peso-\ndenominated government bonds, the annex considers the impact of pension funds in the market. With\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 587,
      "end_char": 765,
      "text": "the recent pension reforms, these funds are expected to play an increasingly important role.\n1. Foreigners (nonresident investors) have been reducing their holdings of domestic,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 765,
      "end_char": 952,
      "text": "peso-denominated government securities since early 2015. The decline was recently in part\ndriven by China’s entry to the global bond index and a consequent loss of Mexico’s weight. While\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 952,
      "end_char": 1148,
      "text": "generally gradual, the reductions temporarily accelerated during periods of increasing risk aversion,\nincluding in the COVID-19 pandemic. Despite the implied capital outflows, the peso and local-\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1148,
      "end_char": 1327,
      "text": "currency government securities markets have remained relatively stable.\nA. Peso Stability\n2. Despite the continued reduction in holdings of\nThe Peso Has Been One of the Strongest\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1327,
      "end_char": 1544,
      "text": "local-currency debt by foreigners over the past year, Performing Currencies Over the Past Year\n1.0 0\nthe peso has performed well, especially compared to\nother emerging market currencies. Even as the trade- 0.8 -5\n-10\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1544,
      "end_char": 1738,
      "text": "weighted dollar index has risen by 8 percent over the last 0.6\n-15\nyear, the peso has followed the dollar’s appreciation.\n0.4\n-20\n“Carry trades” likely explain part of this performance.\n0.2 -25\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1738,
      "end_char": 1951,
      "text": "Banxico’s quick response to rising inflation pressures\npushed the interest rate spread versus the U.S. to over 6 0.0 -30\npercent, close to previous highs in the past decade or so.\n3-Month Carry/Implied Volatility\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1951,
      "end_char": 2176,
      "text": "Compared to other emerging markets, Mexico now has a\nFX Performance since June 1, 2021 (RHS)\nrelatively high ratio of carry to implied volatility, a\nSource: Bloomberg.\ncommon metric used to assess the attractiveness of carry\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2176,
      "end_char": 2371,
      "text": "trades. Nevertheless, there seems to be little correlation between recent currency performance and\nthis measure, with countries with even higher ratios performing worse than the peso, suggesting\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2371,
      "end_char": 2572,
      "text": "that there have been other drivers behind the recent peso stability. Mexico’s relatively strong policy\nframework is likely an important factor, as well as a recovery in other flows. Notably, the post-\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2572,
      "end_char": 2746,
      "text": "pandemic recovery in the U.S. and easing of COVID-19 restrictions have helped drive a strong\nrecovery in remittances and tourism.\nB. Calm Domestic Government Security Market\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2746,
      "end_char": 2920,
      "text": "3. The continued sales by foreigners have not had the important adverse impact on\nconditions and liquidity in markets for domestic government securities like they had in the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2920,
      "end_char": 3136,
      "text": "past. Instead, markets have remained relatively calm, as domestic investors have been filling\nthe absence of foreigners. Pension funds in particular have been buying domestic government\nINTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 67"
    }
  ]
}

